rch + memmove is O(k). For small k (k up to maybe 100?), both are fine. But the problem statement doesn't specify typical k; it's input-dependent. However, we can try.\n\nHypothesis: \"Replacing max-heap insertion with sorted array and binary-search+memmove reduces branch mispredictions and instruction count for heap maintenance, while NEON SIMD distance computation remains unchanged. This improves overall runtime by eliminating the logarithmic overhead of heap operations.\"\n\nTransformations:\n- Replace HeapNode array with a simple float dists[] and char labels[] arrays.\n- Maintain sorted order (descending) so that largest is at index 0.\n- For each new distance, if less than current max or not full yet, find insertion point via binary search on distances, then memmove to shift elements right by one.\n- Use NEON as before for batch computation.\n\nBut we must ensure correctness: the final labels are sorted and frequency counted. The order of labels in the array doesn't matter as long as we have k smallest distances (or largest? Actually we want k nearest = smallest distances). In max-heap, root is maximum among kept; if new distance < root, replace. So we keep k smallest distances. With sorted descending array, index 0 holds largest of the k smallest. Insertion condition: if not full or d < dists[0], then insert.\n\nWe need to implement binary search on float array. Use standard bsearch? But we can write inline function.\n\nPotential issue: memmove for each insertion may be costly if k is large (e.g., 1000). But typical k might be small (like 5 in example). The problem statement doesn't specify bounds; but it's a marathon problem, likely moderate. We'll assume it's acceptable.\n\nWe must also handle the case where heap_size < k: just insert at end and bubble up? Actually with sorted array we can simply insert at correct position using binary search and shift right.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Allocate float dists[k] and char labels[k].\n- Keep int size = 0.\n- For each distance d, if size < k or d < dists[0], then find insertion index via binary search on the sorted array (descending order). Then memmove(&dists[pos+1], &dists[pos], (size - pos)*sizeof(float)) and similarly for labels. Insert at pos.\n- After processing all points, we have k smallest distances in descending order? Actually if we always maintain descending order with largest first, then after insertion of a smaller distance, it goes to the end or somewhere; but we want ascending order for frequency counting later? The current code uses qsort on labels array (which are unsorted) and then counts frequencies. With sorted array, we can directly count frequencies without sorting again because labels will be in same order as distances? Not necessarily: if multiple points have same distance from different groups, the label ordering may not be monotonic with respect to group. But frequency counting only cares about label values; we need to sort labels after extracting them. So we still need qsort on labels array (or we can count frequencies directly by scanning sorted distances? No, because labels are independent of distance order). Actually if we maintain the k smallest points in an array where each entry has dist and label, we could then extract labels into a separate array and sort that as before. That's fine.\n\nBut note: The current code extracts labels from heap (which is not sorted by label) and then qsort them. With our approach, we can similarly copy labels to a temporary array and qsort. So no change there.\n\nPotential performance gain: binary search + memmove may be faster than heap operations for small k due to better cache behavior and fewer branches? Hard to know. But it's worth trying.\n\nWe must ensure that the NEON batch loop remains unchanged except we call new insertion function instead of heap_insert_or_replace.\n\nAlso, we need to handle remainder scalar loop similarly.\n\nLet's write code:\n\nIn knn.c:\n- Remove HeapNode struct and heap funct