1 and only set when inserted.\n\nSimplify: allocate float dists[k] (uninitialized) and char lab_arr[k]; for i in 0..k-1 lab_arr[i]=-1; int size=0.\nDuring insertion, if size<k or d < dists[0], then find pos, shift right from pos to min(size,k-1), insert at pos. If size<k, increment size.\n\nAfter all points, we have lab_arr with k entries (some -1). Then qsort(lab_arr, k, sizeof(char), compare_for_sort) as before.\n\nWe must be careful: when shifting for insertion and size==k, we need to drop the largest element (at index 0? Actually if descending order, root is at index 0. When inserting a smaller distance, it will go somewhere; the largest may shift out if we insert at position >0? But since we only insert if d < dists[0], and array size is k, after insertion we still have k elements: we remove the current largest (index 0) by shifting left? Actually our algorithm: find pos where to insert. Then memmove from pos+1 to end shifts right; but that would overwrite index k-1 if size==k? We need to drop one element. Since array is full, inserting a new smaller distance means we must discard the largest (index 0). But with descending order, the largest is at index 0. If we insert somewhere else, the largest remains unless we shift it out. To maintain k smallest distances, when size==k and d < dists[0], we should replace the root (largest) with new distance and then reorder? That's similar to heap but simpler: we can just set dists[0]=d, lab_arr[0]=label, and then bubble down by comparing with children? Not trivial.\n\nAlternative approach: maintain array in ascending order (smallest first). Then root is at index 0. Insertion condition: if size<k or d < dists[k-1] (largest), then insert at correct position to keep ascending order, shifting right and dropping the last element if full. That might be easier because we can just shift from insertion point to end; if full, the largest at index k-1 gets overwritten by shift? Actually if array is sorted ascending, dists[0] smallest, dists[k-1] largest. When inserting a new distance d that is smaller than current max (dists[k-1]), we find position pos where to insert such that order remains ascending. Then memmove from pos+1 to k-1 shifts right; the element at index k-1 moves out of array? But if size==k, after shift, the last element would be duplicated? We need to drop the largest (index k-1). So we can do: if size == k and d < dists[k-1], then find pos, memmove(&dists[pos+1], &dists[pos], (k-pos-1)*sizeof(float)) which shifts elements from pos to k-2 right by one; then set dists[pos]=d. This effectively discards the last element (largest). That works.\n\nSo I'll use ascending order array: smallest distances first, largest at end. Insertion condition: if size < k or d < dists[size-1] (when full, compare with max). Then find insertion index via binary search for ascending order: while(low<=high){ mid=(low+high)/2; if(dists[mid] <= d) low=mid+1; else high=mid-1; } After loop, low is first element > d. Insert at low.\n\nImplementation details:\n\nstatic inline int insert_sorted_asc(float d, char label, float *dists, char *labels, int size, int k) {\n    if (size == 0) {\n        dists[0] = d; labels[0] = label;\n        return 1;\n    }\n    // If full and d >= current max, skip\n    if (size == k && d >= dists[k-1]) return size;\n    \n    int low = 0, high = size - 1;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int mid = (low + high) / 2;\n        if (dists[mid] <= d)\n            low = mid + 1; // go right\n        else\n            high = mid - 1;\n    }\n    int pos = low; // insertion point: first element > d, or size if all <= d\n    \n    // If full and we are inserting a smaller distance, we will drop the largest (last)\n    int new_size = size < k ? size + 1 : k;\n    \n    // Shift elements from pos to size-2 right by one\n    int shift_count = (size - pos);\n    if (shift_count > 0) {\n        memmove(&dists[pos+1], &dists[pos], shift_count * sizeof(float));\n        memmove(&labels[pos+1]